  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing P. vivax malaria, radical cure completed (B51.9)</content>. Course completed without hemolysis: hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL, platelets 212 x10^3/uL and bilirubin 0.7 mg/dL, all improved. The smear today is negative; the PCR is sent and will be reported by telephone. Radical cure is complete; relapse is now unlikely but possible, so surveillance continues. [S]<list><item>Keep the fever action plan, including during travel and after return.</item><item>No further malaria tests unless he has a fever.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count and bilirubin for hemolysis on primaquine</td><td>Baseline, then after the course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL, platelets 212 x10^3/uL, bilirubin 0.7 mg/dL: no hemolysis</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Follow-up blood smear after treatment (ideally within one week of finishing)</td><td>About one week after finishing</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Smear negative today; PCR sent. Protocol deviation: the smear and PCR ordered for one week after the last primaquine dose were not drawn, because no date was booked; they were drawn today, two weeks after finishing. No harm resulted: the smear is negative and he has had no fever. Corrective action: book post-treatment tests with a date when treatment starts</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects: nausea, abdominal pain, dark urine, breathlessness, cyanosis</td><td>During the course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild nausea on chloroquine and mild cramps on primaquine; no dark urine or cyanosis</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence to the full course</td><td>At the end of the course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>All doses of both medicines taken</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Relapse surveillance and the fever action plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No fever; explained the plan back correctly</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No further malaria relapse in the six months after radical cure. In progress; target 2026-10-13 [S]</item><item>All required and recommended travel vaccines given, with a valid yellow fever certificate, at least ten days before departure. In progress; target 2026-10-13 [S]</item><item>Knows to have a malaria test the same day for any fever, during travel and for months after return. Outcome by 2026-03-31: Carries a fever card and explained the plan back correctly [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Treatment of Uncomplicated Malaria (clinical guidance) (2026); CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel, 2026 edition. Malaria (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in three weeks. Pre-travel visit in the summer. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-21: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
